>
    <p>Most of the businesses we represent are owned by a handful of people who know each other well: founders, families, two partners and a silent investor. The law they need is rarely dramatic. It is the entity that suits their taxes and their plans, an agreement that says who decides what, and contracts that do not need a lawyer to explain them later.</p>
    <p>We work alongside your accountant from the first meeting, because the choice of entity is as much a tax question as a legal one. And we write for the day something changes: a partner leaves, a buyer calls, a founder retires. Those are the days a business finds out whether its paperwork was any good.</p>
